A||||5||theogre||I've drowned in a sea of parts.||||1||
Z||000000||theogre||04-15-2000||03:16 PM||||Went to the scrap yard today. Note to self: just split the hinges. Don't waste time with skinning the door. Especially on power windows and no battery.<P>I got all kind of fun things. Leather steering wheel. Dash with No cracks. the wiring for the doors. The electric doors. back console in really good shape. The light sockets for the low courtesy lights tons of screws and other odd bits. <P>Know what I'll be doing over the next few clear weekends.<P>Does anyone know where the trunk light connects to the harness in the 86? I traced it to the left side where it went under the clip. Seemed like allot of effort for a light that's near useless allot of the time. MAybe I'll do a light on the deck lid. That would light the whole back.||||reg||1
Z||000001||fieroshop||04-15-2000||05:30 PM||||Look on the lid. On the lock mechanism there is a switch, it controls the trunk light. That is plugged in by the battery where you can easily access it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gokart||04-15-2000||08:15 PM||||Hey Ogre, that's a great idea! My whole light assembly fell down into the firewall but I remember from the 2M4 I used to have how useless it was anyway. Some kind of bright "touch" light mounted on the deck lid fitted so it's in the trunk away from the heat when shut. I like it!||||reg||14
Z||000003||Bill Osmer||04-15-2000||10:48 PM||||I've done this by adding surface-mounted generic-style auxillary lights, and I'm not sorry. It does take running an extra wire (a battery source) to the new lights for them to work. Apparently, the switch at the latch only switches ground. Anyway, the ones that I got have there own built-in switch so that I can turn them off when I just want the stock look.||||reg||2
Z||000004||theogre||04-16-2000||10:36 PM||||Fshop..... I know how the light is activated. It's the light itself I wanted to know about.<P>The wire goes from the light to a grommit on the left side of the front trunk wall. Once thru the grommit the leads go around the strut tower to the left side of the clip. Then they disappear under the clip.<P>I don't think I'll waste my time on it. I'll just use a light on/in the lid like I was thinking above. Then I can see the whole engine bay at night and the lights not shining in my eyes like the one down in the trunk does when on.<P>Maybe something like the one Bats put in If he'll ever tell us what the heck he got it from.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DJRice||04-17-2000||09:46 AM||||Ogre, <P>If I recall correctly, and its been a while since I have looked at the parts car I dismantled the rear end of, the trunk light ends up connected to the harness that feeds the tail lights.  That entire harness connects to the main block mounted near the battery.  <P>That light is pretty useless especially if you have a suitcase in the trunk of the car.  A deck lid mounted light would be far more functional.||||reg||1
